  "tour": {
    "title": "Hesed: Loyal Love in Action",
    "intro": "This tour starts by untangling the claim that hesed is “untranslatable,” then follows the word through Exodus 34, Psalm 36, Ruth, David’s treatment of Mephibosheth, and Micah 6:8. You’ll hear why no single English gloss is enough—and how commitment, affection, generosity, faithfulness, and justice belong together in different contexts.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 757,
        "endSec": 1008,
        "why": "Begin here for the working definition. The hosts say hesed is love shown while “keeping a promise,” with a willingness to go “above and beyond and be super generous”; their elderly-spouse illustration makes clear why it is more than a feeling or a bare duty. This gives you a flexible core before the examples.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:c416445833e65b5f726afb158ec92353",
        "startSec": 1440,
        "endSec": 1651,
        "why": "This grounds the word in its most famous Old Testament self-description. As the episode walks through Exodus 34:6–7, it notes that translations range from “covenant loyalty” and “covenant faithfulness” to love, then gives Luther’s memorable phrase “goodness in action.” It lets you hear hesed as God’s active, enduring goodness rather than a vague emotion.",
        "episodeTitle": "Episode 105 (Exodus 33:18-34:16)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_105.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2488,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 1008,
        "endSec": 1208,
        "why": "Now hear hesed in Hebrew poetry. In Psalm 36 the speakers place “Your chesed, O Lord, reaches to the skies” beside faithfulness, righteousness, and justice, and say God’s character “upholds every tier of the cosmos.” This widens the word from a private kindness to a sweeping description of God’s reliable character.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:a88b1fb7-fd1c-415f-b24c-98322c767992",
        "startSec": 928,
        "endSec": 1125,
        "why": "Ruth shows the word moving through ordinary human loyalty. Naomi asks Yahweh to “deal kindly” with her daughters-in-law, and the hosts call this “covenant faithfulness,” then identify Ruth’s decision to remain with Naomi and Yahweh as “an example of Hesed love.” This is a good test case because it includes grief, kinship, costly allegiance, and a Moabite outsider.",
        "episodeTitle": "Ruth 1-2: God Fills What's Empty",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG9761838334.mp3?updated=1664486106",
        "durationSec": 3645,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:478e6896-2d24-4806-ac5f-4db234766229",
        "startSec": 1582,
        "endSec": 2009,
        "why": "This is the most concrete narrative payoff. David does not merely feel kindly toward Jonathan’s surviving family; he asks to show “the kindness of God,” restores Mephibosheth’s land, and gives him a permanent place at the king’s table. The commentators explicitly say the act reveals “what David believes the kindness of God is like,” showing hesed as covenant loyalty expressed through extravagant generosity.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Samuel 8:9-9:13 (Episode 267)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_Min_ep_667.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2471,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 1500,
        "endSec": 1678,
        "why": "End with the prophetic challenge: Micah’s “to do justice, to love chesed, and to walk humbly” is not a synonym list, the hosts say, but a call to covenant loyalty, justice for the needy and poor, and humility. Most importantly, they emphasize, “Chesed is not a feeling. Chesed is a visible action.” This keeps the word from becoming a decorative translation or a private spiritual mood.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips suggest that hesed is not impossible to translate; it is impossible to compress into one English word. Depending on the relationship and scene, “steadfast love,” “loving kindness,” “loyal love,” “covenant faithfulness,” or “mercy” may foreground part of it. The common thread is committed love made visible in faithful, generous action—toward God, neighbor, outsider, and the vulnerable.",
    "totalSeconds": 1464
  },
